#!/usr/bin/env python3
# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""
https-ssh - run SSH over plain HTTP/HTTPS.

Why: Cloudflare Tunnel (cloudflared) only speaks HTTP, so a plain `ssh` to a
tunnelled host does not work. This service listens on a local HTTP port,
authenticates the POSTed credentials against the real sshd, and then carries the
SSH session over a websocket - the one streaming transport Cloudflare supports.

Layout of the two sides
-----------------------
  client  --HTTPS-->  Cloudflare  --HTTP-->  https-ssh serve  --TCP-->  sshd
  (ssh)                                      (127.0.0.1:8899)          (localhost)

Auth model
----------
  * POST /login {host, port, user, password|key} runs the REAL `ssh` binary
    against the target and returns a token ONLY if sshd accepted the credentials.
    Whatever sshd accepts, this gateway accepts - no second password database.
  * The token is bound to (user, host, port). /tunnel and /exec can only reach
    that exact target, so a token can never be used as an open relay.
  * /tunnel is a websocket that carries raw sshd bytes, which makes it usable
    with `ssh -o ProxyCommand` - plain ssh, scp, sftp and -L forwarding all work.

Subcommands
-----------
  serve                     run the HTTP gateway (this is the "service")
  login  USER@HOST          verify credentials via POST /login, store the token
  connect USER@HOST         login (if needed) + open a real interactive ssh
  proxy  HOST PORT          websocket tunnel client, used as ssh ProxyCommand
  exec   USER@HOST -- CMD   run one command over HTTPS only (no local ssh)
  status | logout           inspect / drop stored tokens

Only the Python standard library is used. The real `ssh` binary does the actual
authentication, so whatever sshd accepts, this accepts.
"""

